A tinySA, as a second opinion
=============================

tinysa.py reads a tinySA over its USB serial line.  It is here for one
reason: os/inferno/radio/cc1120.c and the qemu model it was developed
against were written by the same hand, and a receiver reporting its
own signal strength is a board agreeing with itself.  A tinySA is an
instrument somebody else built, with its own front end, its own
calibration and its own reasons to be wrong.

	tools/tinysa/tinysa.py sweep 868000000 928000000 >tsa.txt
	tools/tinysa/tinysa.py cmd version rbw attenuate

and on the board, over the same band:

	; echo scan >/dev/radio0/ctl

Both are frequency-and-level, so the comparison is awk(1).  The two do
not measure the same thing and are not expected to agree in absolute
terms: a tinySA ULTRA at 100kHz resolution bandwidth and no input
attenuation is a calibrated receiver, while the CC1120's RSSI is its
own AGC's estimate with the manufacturer's reference offset applied
and no calibration of this board at all.  What is worth comparing is
where the energy is and whether it stays there, not how much of it
there is.

The reference instrument needs checking too
-------------------------------------------

The first thing this tool measured was nothing at all, convincingly.

The tinySA stops sweeping while it serves commands on the serial line,
and "data 0" hands back whatever is in the trace buffer.  If nothing
has swept since, that is the previous sweep -- or the sweep of
whatever span the thing was showing before the range was set.  Read
that way it looks exactly like a measurement.  Three consecutive reads
agreed to the last digit: r = 1.000 between every pair, 0 of 450
points differing, a confident plateau at 868.13 to 868.80MHz at -71 to
-77dBm.

None of which was in the band it appeared to be in, because the trace
predated the "sweep 868000000 928000000" that was supposed to have
produced it.

What caught it was the same test the board's own sweeps had to pass: a
repeat.  Thermal noise measured twice does not agree at r = 1.000.
Every trace is now taken with resume, wait, pause around it, which
runs exactly one sweep and stops at the end of it, and two sweeps of
the same quiet band through the tool now read r = 0.287 with 35 of 450
points alike, which is what a repeat is supposed to look like.

There is a lesson in that worth more than the measurement: an
instrument brought in to check another instrument is not automatically
the more trustworthy of the two.

The tinySA's own formatter, at -100.0dBm exactly
------------------------------------------------

A trace containing a level of exactly -100.0dBm has a line reading

	-:.000000e+01

in it.  ':' is the character after '9': the mantissa rounded up to
10.0 and the digit carried off the end of the digit set.  It is
reproducible on every read of such a trace and is not a serial fault,
which matters, because a reader that drops malformed lines silently
loses a point from the sweep exactly where the quietest bins are.
Read as written it is -10.000000e+01, which is the value it meant, and
tinysa.py reads it that way.

What was measured, 22 September 2026
------------------------------------

Six sweeps of 868 to 928MHz, 450 points each, 100kHz resolution
bandwidth, no input attenuation, taken minutes after the board's own
sweeps of the same band from the same bench.

	Floor: -100.4dBm, the median of all 2700 points.
	No bin anywhere in the band is above -90dBm in all six sweeps.
	59 of the 450 bins are above -88dBm in at least one sweep.
	No bin is above it in more than two of the six.
	Each sweep's loudest point is between -69 and -80dBm, and is
	at a different frequency every time: 886.17, 873.75, 871.61,
	886.17, 885.11, 869.60MHz.
	The 59 bins run from 869.47 to 889.38MHz and stop there; the
	upper half of the band, 890 to 928, has none.

So: a quiet band with short bursts in it, scattered in frequency,
never twice in the same place, and confined to the lower half.

The board said the same thing.  Its sweep of the same band found 15
steps of 61 with a peak 28dB above a flat floor and carrier sense
asserted, and the next sweep minutes later found none of them, with
the peak of every step landing at a random point in its dwell rather
than at the start.  Two receivers with nothing in common but the
antenna port they are pointed at -- different front ends, different
calibration, different sweep mechanics, different software by
different people -- both report a quiet floor with intermittent
bursts at scattered frequencies.

That is as much as either can say.  Neither can identify what is
transmitting, and this is not a survey: six sweeps of a minute's
worth of time from one bench.  What it is good for is the thing it was
brought in for -- the board's radio is measuring the room, not
reporting its own imagination.
